{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>וַיְהִי.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Megillah 10a) that it is an expression of distress — it alludes to the evil event that befell the tablets, that the letters flew off them, as they said of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 46:1). And this caused us everything that our fathers tasted and we too taste of the bitter cups — death itself and what is more bitter than death — for had the first tablets endured, destruction would have ceased from the world and freedom even from the angel of death (Shemot Rabbah 41:7), but now a good thing had ceased. Therefore it is fitting to call it <b>וַיְהִי</b>. It further alludes as well to the distress of that righteous man, Moses our master, upon him be peace, who suffered greatly over what he saw. It further alludes as well to Israel, who were found in the shame of a thief, for they were found in great embarrassment when Moses drew near the camp. It further alludes as well to the aspect of the kelippah, to which the ״וי״ (woe) drew near to agitate it and to destroy its animation and the evil spirituality within it, which is what was said of it: ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — the bitterness of death departed from it; so may the enemies of HaShem perish." "<b>כַּאֲשֶׁר קָרַב.</b> Perhaps Scripture is informing us that sight of the calf was arranged before Moses before he entered the camp, and the reason was so that the written tablets would not enter a camp in which there is idolatry. And this is the sense of the verse: <b>וַיְהִי כַּאֲשֶׁר קָרַב</b> — immediately and at once <b>וַיַּרְא אֶת הָעֵגֶל</b>, ready before him." "<b>וַיַּרְא אֶת הָעֵגֶל וּמְחוֹלוֹת.</b> Perhaps, precisely at the moment of Moses' approach, the aspect of impurity that had been speaking from within it and saying ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ flew away from it — and likewise the spirit of impurity that had formed its shape without a smith or craftsman immediately departed at the sight of Moses, for the kelippah recognizes its master and trembles and hides. And therefore it says <b>וַיַּרְא אֶת הָעֵגֶל וּמְחוֹלוֹת</b>, likening the calf to the dances: just as the dances are inanimate, so too the calf is inanimate, for the misleading spirit had passed out of it." "<b>אוֹ</b> it may say, examining the precision of its saying <b>אֶת הָעֵגֶל</b> — that it should not have needed to say anything other than ״וַיַּרְא הָעֵגֶל״ — rather, it intended to say that he saw the calf and the spirit of impurity within it, for the righteous recognize the aspect of evil, and all the more so a man such as Moses upon him be peace, who ascended and trod upon the heights of all spirits when he went up to Elohim. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Tikkunim 142) that Moses asked the calf who made it, and it answered that the mixed multitude made it — one may examine their words there, and the matter is straightforward, for an inanimate thing does not speak. And you will find that regarding the dances it did not say ״אֶת,״ because there was no spirit of impurity chattering in them as there was in the calf, according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory cited above." "<b>אוֹ</b> it further intends to inform us that at the time Moses drew near the camp, no person appeared before him because of the embarrassment, and he saw nothing other than the calf and the dances; but because of it, those who held the dance cleared the place. And therefore it says <b>וַיַּרְא אֶת הָעֵגֶל וּמְחֹלֹת</b>, to say that this alone was what he saw." "<b>And the anger of Moses burned, and he cast.</b> One needs to know the reason of Moses for breaking the tablets, and he did not concern himself with an extraordinary loss; and it is obvious that he would not do a destructive thing unless with great reckoning and from sides of benefit upon which he, peace be upon him, relied. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot de-Rabbi Nathan 2:3) that he did not break until it was said to him from the mouth of the Almighty, and so said Rabbi Meir: ״וַיִּהְיוּ שָׁם כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוַּנִי״ (Deuteronomy 10:5) — meaning he was commanded and broke the tablets; this far. And one needs to give a reason to El Elyon, why He withheld the good." "And it appears that before they served idolatry, their zuhamah (spiritual impurity) had ceased, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 146a); and therefore HaShem wrought for them a judgment inscribed חָרוּת (engraved) upon the tablets — חֵרוּת (freedom) from the Angel of Death (Shemot Rabbah 41), for as it were, this death was only for the purpose of separating the zuhamah, but if their zuhamah had already ceased. And therefore when they made the Calf, behold the zuhamah of sin cleaved to them, and from now on they need the Angel of Death. Even though the zuhamah did not return in actuality through the Calf to the degree it had been before the reception of the Torah, nevertheless He broke them, for this judgment no longer applies to them — that they should be free men from the Angel of Death and from the subjugation of kingdoms. And according to this reason, even if it were said that Moses acted from his own understanding at first, there is no difficulty here."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :register {:flags {5 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:23.382622505Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}